    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the string `membershipTier` with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133) computed at creation.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Replaced `membershipTier(Owner)` (the old GOLD/SILVER/BRONZE logic) with `public static int membershipLevel(Owner)`: starts at 1, `+1` when an email is present, `+1` when `namesakeCount` is 0, capped at 3 (level 4 reserved for tenure). Updated the `@Mapping` to target `membershipLevel`.\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier` (string) schema property with `membershipLevel` (`integer`/`int32`, min 1, max 3, read-only), so the generated `OwnerDto` now exposes `membershipLevel` and no longer has `membershipTier`.\n\n3. **`AuditOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 The create audit line now records `membershipLevel=` (via `OwnerMapper.membershipLevel(owner)`) instead of the tier. Verified in the run output: `AUDIT - owner created: id=11 customerCode=TOW-LEV-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-07 membershipLevel=3`.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreNumericLevel` (asserting `$.membershipLevel == 3` for an owner with an email and no namesakes) passes, and the full suite is green.\n\nOne design note: I made `membershipLevel` a single `static` method on `OwnerMapper` so both the response mapping and the audit line derive the value from one source of truth. Both inputs (email from the request, `namesakeCount` set by `AssignNamesakeCount`) are already on the entity before the audit and respond steps run.",
